#basePath Day0

::OpeningPrelude
@trigger    SceneReady
@boostScore 100
@who    Astrid
{@astrid} Come on, Elijah, where are you? It’s already starting!
 
$call Wait(1)
(*phone buzzing sfx*)
 
{@Elijah} Astrid, I’m so sorry I’m late! There was something going on at the lab, some weird anomaly—

{@Astrid} Ooh, that sounds mysterious.
Did they discover a new comet or something?
 
{@Elijah} I don’t know. Told them I had a party to get to.
 
{@Astrid} Good. The Perseids aren’t gonna wait forever.
 
{@Elijah} Wow . . . it’s incredible.
 
{@Astrid} Yeah. It never gets old.
 
{@Elijah} Just think . . . next year on your birthday we’ll be astronomy majors in college, watching the meteor shower together. I hear UW has some great stargazing spots.
 
{@Astrid} My aunt doesn’t think we’ll be together next year.

{@Elijah}What?
$call Wait(1)
Why not? 
$call Wait(1)
 
{@Astrid} She says it won’t last. Things change. 
 
{@Elijah} Of course things change. So what? We can still be together.
Trust me, Astrid. It’s written in the stars. 
$call Wait(2)
Did you read my birthday card?


{@Elijah} “Set me as a seal upon your heart, as a seal upon your arm.”
$call Wait(1)
Sorry . . . too cheesy?

{@Astrid} Maybe a little. But I liked it. 
Who’s the drawing supposed to be?
 
{@Elijah} It’s you! I drew you as Perseus.
 
{@Astrid} Like . . . the constellation?
 
{@Elijah} Exactly. It’s based on a Greek myth.
This meteor shower is called the Perseids because Perseus is its radiant.
You know . . . the point in the sky where the meteor shower looks like it’s starting. 
 
{@Astrid} Wow . . . I’ve watched the Perseids every year since I was six, and I never knew that.
I guess Dad and I were more focused on finding Ursa Major and Ursa Minor. I used to call them Papa Bear and Little Bear. 
Is Perseus visible from here?
 
{@Elijah} Sure. It’s easy to find. 
Just look for the place where the meteors seem to be coming from.
If you move your phone across the sky, it’ll tell you if you’re getting warmer or colder.
 
 
{@Astrid} Found it!
We’re so lucky to have the sky all mapped out.
It would take forever to figure all this out from scratch.
 
{@Elijah} I don’t know. Sounds sort of fun to me.
 
{@Astrid} Hey, I never said it wouldn’t be fun. 
Now you have to explain. How am I like Perseus?
Didn’t he get cast out to sea by his dad, and then end up becoming some big hero?
 
{@Elijah} Exactly!
He went on a quest, fell in love with the princess Andromeda, and saved her from a sea monster.
 
{@Astrid} Stillll not seeing how that’s like me.
 
{@Elijah} Come on. Isn’t it obvious?
Your dad’s off doing research at the South Pole, so you’ve been cast out to that little town with your aunt. You’re about to start your senior year at a brand new school. And you’re still completely fearless.
 
{@Astrid} My aunt was right. You’re such a hopeless romantic.
 
{@Elijah} Romantic? Sure. But hopeless? Never.
 
{@Astrid} So if I’m Perseus, does that make you Andromeda?
 
{@Elijah} I don’t know. I think I’m more of a—
 
 
{@Astrid} Elijah? Hello?
$call Wait(2) 
Whoa. Elijah, are you seeing this?

{@Elijah} Astrid—you’re breaking up—
 
{@Astrid} Is that the Northern Lights?
Whoa. It’s so bright!
 
{@Elijah} (*bzzzzzzz krrrrrrr*) —Something’s not right—
(*static noises*) —Readings off the charts— (*brrrrrr kzzzzzz*)
 
{@Astrid} Elijah, can you hear me?
$call Wait(2) 
Hello?
$call Wait(3) 
Elijah?

$call LoadNextDay()
